---
import Callout from "../../components/Callout.astro";
import PageShell from "../../components/PageShell.astro";
import MainLayout from "../../layouts/MainLayout.astro";
---

<MainLayout title="AI Rubber Ducking - AI Training Guide">
	<PageShell chapterId="requirements-gathering" subChapterId="rubber-ducking">
		<div class="prose space-y-6">
			<p>
				Rubber ducking is a classic debugging technique: explain your problem to an inanimate object (traditionally a rubber duck) and the act of articulating it helps you find the answer. AI takes this idea and turns it into something genuinely interactive. Instead of a silent duck, you get something that asks questions back.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Question Your Assumptions
			</h3>

			<p>
				One valuable but underused capability is questioning your assumptions and finding missing items. When you describe a feature, the AI can spot gaps you've glossed over because you're too close to the problem. Ask it to do this explicitly.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed italic">
						"Here's what I'm thinking for our new notification system in @path/to/file.md. I want you to poke holes in it. What am I missing? What assumptions am I making that might be wrong? What edge cases haven't I considered?"
					</p>
				</div>

				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">What the AI might surface</div>
					<ul class="list-disc pl-5 space-y-2 text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">
						<li>"What happens when a user has notifications disabled at the OS level?"</li>
						<li>"You mention real-time updates, but what's the fallback if the WebSocket connection drops?"</li>
						<li>"How does this interact with the existing email notification system? Are they complementary or replacement?"</li>
						<li>"You haven't mentioned rate limiting. What happens if a user triggers 500 notifications in a minute?"</li>
					</ul>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				This is a good way to get an outside perspective on your requirements and catch things you might have missed. The AI behaves like a brand-new employee: it knows nothing about the project and is not afraid to ask the obvious questions, so it surfaces things you stopped noticing long ago.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="warning" title="Watch for agreement that comes too easily">
				<p>
					AI models lean toward telling you what you want to hear. If you ask "is this a good plan?" you'll usually get a yes, whether or not it deserves one. That sycophancy is the main thing that undermines rubber ducking, because agreement feels like validation when it is really just the model being agreeable. Force it to disagree: "assume this design fails in production, what broke?", "give me the strongest argument against this approach", or "what would a skeptical senior engineer flag first?". You want the objection you haven't thought of, not a gold star.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Finding Missing Requirements
			</h3>

			<p>
				Beyond poking holes in what you've written, AI is good at spotting what you haven't written. Feed it your requirements document and ask specifically about gaps.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg px-5 py-4">
					<div class="text-xs font-bold text-violet-600 uppercase tracking-wider mb-2">Example prompt</div>
					<p class="text-sm text-stone-700 leading-relaxed italic">
						"I've attached our requirements for the checkout redesign. Review it as if you're a senior PM doing a requirements review. What sections are underdeveloped? What stakeholder concerns might be missing? Are there any non-functional requirements we should add?"
					</p>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				Notice the technique here: assigning the AI a specific role ("senior PM doing a requirements review") gives it a perspective to work from. It'll approach the problem differently than if you just ask "is this good?", which tends to produce vague praise.
			</p>

			<h3>
				Keep It Conversational
			</h3>

			<p>
				Rubber ducking works best as a back-and-forth. Don't try to get everything in one massive prompt. Start with your high-level idea, let the AI ask questions, answer them, and iterate. This mirrors how you'd work through requirements with a colleague.
			</p>

			<div class="space-y-4 my-8">
				<div class="border border-stone-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
					<div class="bg-stone-50 px-5 py-3 border-b border-stone-200">
						<div class="text-xs font-bold text-stone-500 uppercase tracking-wider">Sample conversation flow</div>
					</div>
					<div class="divide-y divide-stone-100">
						<div class="px-5 py-3">
							<div class="text-xs font-semibold text-violet-600 mb-1">You</div>
							<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">We need to add bulk export for our reporting dashboard. Users have been asking for it. What should we consider? and can you make a requirements document for it based on @path/to/template.md</p>
						</div>
						<div class="px-5 py-3 bg-stone-50/50">
							<div class="text-xs font-semibold text-stone-500 mb-1">AI</div>
							<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">A few clarifying questions: What formats do users expect (CSV, PDF, Excel)? Are we talking about exporting the visible data or the full underlying dataset? Is there a row limit we should consider? And should this be synchronous or do we need a background job for large exports?</p>
						</div>
						<div class="px-5 py-3">
							<div class="text-xs font-semibold text-violet-600 mb-1">You</div>
							<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">CSV and Excel. Full dataset. No row limit currently but some clients have 100k+ rows. Probably needs to be async for those.</p>
						</div>
						<div class="px-5 py-3 bg-stone-50/50">
							<div class="text-xs font-semibold text-stone-500 mb-1">AI</div>
							<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">For 100k+ rows async makes sense. That raises a few more things: Where does the generated file get stored? How long do we keep it? How does the user know it's ready — polling, WebSocket notification, email? And do we need to handle the case where someone starts an export and then starts another one before the first finishes?</p>
						</div>
						<div class="px-5 py-3">
							<div class="text-xs font-semibold text-violet-600 mb-1">You</div>
							<p class="text-sm text-stone-600 leading-relaxed">Good catch on the concurrent exports. I hadn't thought about that...</p>
						</div>
					</div>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p>
				In a quick back-and-forth, the AI surfaced storage, retention, notification, and concurrency questions that weren't in the original ask. That's the value of rubber ducking: not that the AI knows your domain better than you, but that it asks the questions you forgot to ask yourself.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="tip" title="Turn this into a repeatable step">
				<p>
					If interrogating your own ideas is this useful, you can make it a one-command habit. Matt Pocock's{" "}
					<a href="https://github.com/mattpocock/skills/tree/main/skills/productivity/grill-me" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">grill-me skill</a>{" "}
					does exactly that: you describe a plan and Claude Code relentlessly interviews you down every branch of the design before writing a line of code. It is rubber ducking packaged as a reusable skill, and it defaults to suggesting its own answer for each question so you are reacting to a concrete proposal rather than a blank prompt.
				</p>
			</Callout>

			<h3>
				Keep It Short
			</h3>

			<p>
				One common trap is letting the AI write pages of analysis when you just need a quick sanity check. Be explicit about what you want back. "Give me a bullet list of concerns, max 5 items" is better than an open-ended "what do you think?" that produces a 2,000-word essay.
			</p>

			<Callout variant="info" title="Remember">
				<p>
					The AI won't remember your conversation next time you open a new chat. If it surfaces valuable insights during rubber ducking, capture them in your actual requirements document before closing the session. This is where <a href="/requirements-gathering/documentation-first-development" class="text-teal-600 hover:text-teal-800">documentation-first</a> pays off: fold what surfaced back into the doc that becomes your spec. The AI is a thinking tool, not a storage tool.
				</p>
			</Callout>
		</div>
	</PageShell>
</MainLayout>
